WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
Traditional server-rendered forms are a core use case for antiforgery protection.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:15.560
Razor Pages and MVC applications often render forms from the server, receive submitted values, validate model state, and then change server-side state.

3
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:20.060
That workflow is exactly where antiforgery validation fits naturally.

4
00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:27.700
ASP.NET Core provides framework conventions and validation attributes that can help apply antiforgery protection.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:32.840
Teams should understand which conventions are active in their application and where explicit attributes are used.

6
00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:41.660
The goal is not to memorize every implementation detail; it is to know which state-changing handlers and actions require valid antiforgery validation.

7
00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:49.540
Consistency matters across full pages, partial views, shared layouts, admin forms, and AJAX form submissions.

8
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:59.320
A form generated through normal helpers may receive expected antiforgery support, while a hand-built form, custom component, or JavaScript submission path may need extra review.

9
00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.620
Different UI paths should not create different protection levels for the same sensitive operation.

10
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.780
Teams should review every unsafe action, not only the most visible forms.

11
00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:23.960
Administrative forms, support tools, account settings, email changes, profile updates, approval workflows, exports, delete operations, and configuration changes can all modify server-side state.

12
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.860
If a browser session can trigger the change, antiforgery behavior should be part of the review.

13
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.180
Forms generated outside normal template conventions deserve careful attention.

14
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:45.080
A shared layout, reusable component, partial view, modal dialog, or custom JavaScript handler may look ordinary to the user while following a different submission path.

15
00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:51.100
Reviewers should trace how the token is rendered, how the request is sent, and where validation happens on the server.

16
00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.100
The practical pattern is to make protection visible.

17
00:01:54.200 --> 00:02:11.160
State-changing Razor Pages and MVC actions should have consistent antiforgery expectations, tests or review notes where appropriate, safe failure behavior, and logging that helps identify configuration or client-flow mistakes without exposing sensitive values.
